From dc8a06711aa9f6782b6d90a5b1080e5a62e348a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:15:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ply-device-manager: Stop watching udev events when freed

ply_device_manager_free() releases the udev monitor and the manager
but leaves the monitor's descriptor watched, with on_udev_event() and
the manager as its handler and data.

plymouthd frees the manager from quit_program(). After "plymouth quit"
with a splash whose become_idle() completes at once, such as the script
plugin, that runs from the idle timeout that ply_boot_splash_t sets.
The event loop runs expired timeouts before it dispatches the
descriptors that epoll_wait() returned in the same pass, and it checks
for an exit request only after each dispatch. When the udev monitor's
descriptor is the first ready one, on_udev_event() runs with the freed
manager and udev_monitor_receive_device() reads the freed monitor.

On an Apple M4 laptop this crashed plymouthd with SIGSEGV in
udev_monitor_receive_device() 5.7 s into boot, twice on record.

Stop watching for udev events before releasing the monitor, as
ply_device_manager_pause() does.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 src/libply-splash-core/ply-device-manager.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/libply-splash-core/ply-device-manager.c b/src/libply-splash-core/ply-device-manager.c
index 016ac332..f8152844 100644
--- a/src/libply-splash-core/ply-device-manager.c
+++ b/src/libply-splash-core/ply-device-manager.c
@@ -926,6 +926,12 @@ ply_device_manager_free (ply_device_manager_t *manager)
                                                   (ply_event_loop_timeout_handler_t)
                                                   create_devices_from_udev, manager);
 
+        /* The monitor's descriptor may already be ready in this pass of the
+         * event loop, for instance when a timeout handler frees the manager.
+         * Its handler must not run with the freed manager and monitor.
+         */
+        stop_watching_for_udev_events (manager);
+
         if (manager->udev_monitor != NULL)
                 udev_monitor_unref (manager->udev_monitor);
 
